---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ConnectionStringBuilder from '../../components/ConnectionStringBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Connection String Builder" description="Build a connection string for PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, or Redis from individual fields.">
  <ConnectionStringBuilder client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why build it this way</h2>
  <p>Manually assembling a connection string means remembering the exact format for each database — user:password@host:port/db, with URL-safe special characters. This tool handles the format so you don't have to look it up each time.</p>

  <h2>Common Connection String Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not URL-encoding special characters</strong> in passwords (like @ or :), which can break the connection string's parsing entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Committing connection strings with real credentials</strong> to version control instead of using environment variables.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Connection String Builder</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Building a connection string for a new database configuration</li>
    <li>Getting the correct format for a database type you don't use often</li>
    <li>Constructing a connection string for a local development environment</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Connection String FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Why does my password need URL encoding?</strong> Special characters like @, :, or / have structural meaning in a connection string URL — if your password contains them literally, the parser can misread where the password ends and the host begins.</p>
  <p><strong>Does this work for cloud-hosted databases?</strong> Yes — enter your cloud provider's hostname and port as given in their connection details; the format itself doesn't change based on where the database is hosted.</p>
</ToolLayout>
